          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Asthma with an acute exacerbation: is that one code, or a code plus something else?",
          "answer": "Asthma with an acute exacerbation is coded using ICD-10 as J45, which is specifically designated for asthma. However, when documenting asthma, it is important to also specify the type or severity, as it may require additional codes to clarify the nature of the asthma being treated.\n\nAcute exacerbation and status asthmaticus are considered exclusive alternative terms in coding. This means that if the document refers to \"status asthmaticus\", it does not also refer to \"acute exacerbation\". When either is documented, it should be represented accurately in the coding. Proper documentation is essential, as it determines the specific code selected for asthma in both scenarios (J45 for asthma and J46 for status asthmaticus) (source: \"Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes\").\n\nAdditionally, if there are other complicating factors present, such as respiratory failure, then those would also require additional codes (source: \"Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44 and its two complication codes\").\n\nIn summary, coding for asthma with acute exacerbation typically involves the J45 code, and depending on the documentation, there may be a need to include other codes that specify the type of asthma or associated conditions.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
